TI  - Aplicação de modelos de Machine Learning para previsão de casos de dengue e classificação de risco epidêmico na região sul do brasil
PY  - 2026
JO  - Revista Vincci - Periódico Científico do UniSATC
AB  - Dengue is an infectious disease with a strong impact on Brazilian public health, where its incidence is directly related to environmental, climatic, and population factors. Predicting outbreaks still represents a major challenge, but advances in the field of data science have enabled new predictive approaches. Among these, machine learning models stand out as effective tools for identifying complex patterns and supporting strategic decision-making in public health. This work aims to develop, evaluate, and compare different machine learning models for predicting dengue outbreaks in Southern Brazil, based on epidemiological data and complementary variables such as climate (temperature, humidity, precipitation) and population characteristics (density, age distribution, and urbanrural distribution). The adopted methodology included processing, normalizing, and integrating different databases, creating derived variables (such as proportions and time lags), applying supervised regression models, and a comparative performance analysis using metrics such as Mean Absolute Error (MAE), Root Mean Square Error (RMSE), and the Coefficient of Determination ($R^2$). Furthermore, the results were evaluated from a risk classification perspective, based on incidence ranges of dengue cases per municipality and epidemiological week, applying metrics such as accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-Score. The results showed significant differences between the tested models. XGBoost, for example, presented the best overall performance, with low mean error (MAE = 7.06) and a high data understanding capacity ($R^2 = 0.94$), in addition to good performance in risk classification (accuracy = 0.926; F1-score = 0.839). Similarly, Random Forest also achieved good results, with a balance between error and accuracy (MAE = 7.80; $R^2 = 0.93$; accuracy = 0.933; F1-score = 0.855). In contrast, simpler models such as Linear Regression and Decision Trees showed lower performance. It is concluded that predictive modeling, when well-founded and contextualized, can be a valuable tool in tackling seasonal diseases such as dengue.
