AU  - L. A. Gonzales
TI  - DengueAI Comparative Time-Series and Deep-Learning Modeling for Early Prediction of Dengue Outbreaks in Rural Peru Using Open Data
PY  - 2025
AB  - Timely forecasting of dengue outbreaks is crucial for directing limited public-health resources in rural, low-income regions. We present DengueAI, an open-source framework that benchmarks classical time-series models against deep-learning architectures using > 25 years of nationwide open data from Peru. Weekly dengue notifications (1999–2024) were enriched with meteorological covariates and analysed with four families of predictors: ARIMA, Prophet, Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) and Bidirectional LSTM (BiLSTM). In short-range horizons (1–4 weeks) the best ARIMA configuration achieved $R^{2}=0.9707$ and MAE = 88.7 cases, outperforming neural networks by 5–8 %, whereas LSTM models captured medium-term, non-linear trends when multiple climate variables were included. All models are deployed in a lightweight Streamlit dashboard that generates forecasts, heat maps and downloadable artefacts for local health offices. The study confirms that well-tuned statistical baselines can equal or surpass deep networks in data-scarce settings, while hybrid strategies offer a practical path toward early-warning systems in underserved areas.
