TI  - Leveraging Climate Data for Dengue Forecasting in San Juan: An Advanced Deep Learning Approach
PY  - 2025
AB  - To achieve timely and accurate prediction of dengue fever cases, this study introduces a one-dimensional convolutional neural network (Conv1D) to facilitate the extraction of local temporal features, and designs a dual attention mechanism to achieve synergistic enhancement between meteorologically sensitive factors and critical epidemic phases. Four different long short-term memory (LSTM)-based models using lagged meteorological features were developed for dengue fever prediction: LSTM, Conv1D-LSTM using one-dimensional convolutional neural network, CT-LSTM with dual attention mechanism, and Conv1D-CT-LSTM using both. These models are trained and evaluated on weekly dengue case data from San Juan spanning 1991 to 2008, using various meteorological variables, lagged meteorological data, and historical dengue case data to predict subsequent weekly case counts. Experimental results demonstrate that models incorporating lagged meteorological features generally outperform those without such features, with further performance improvements observed when using Conv1D and the dual attention mechanism. Among these, the Conv1D-CTLSTM model using lagged meteorological features performed best, with $\mathrm{R}^{2}$, MAE, and MSE scores of 0.88, 6.77, and 122.60, respectively. Overall, the Conv1D-CT-LSTM model proved to be the most effective for predicting dengue fever cases in San Juan.
